\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The goal of this talk is,
 ostensibly,
 to talk about 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-randomness,
 but in practice I'm going to be ranting at length about this one proposition in Denis' book that confused me.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
This talk will be the talk I wish someone had given to me before I read this proposition.
 This would have helped me understand it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The proposition in question is Theorem 6.8.3,
 and we'll get to what it says in full glory later,
 but first,
 I want to give context.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
In his last class,
 Denis talked about the notion of weak 2-randomness,
 which is stronger than 1-randomness AKA Martin-Löf randomness.
 In Section 6.8 of his book,
 Denis and Rod Downey introduce the notion of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-randomness,
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\N$
\end_inset

.
 The path they trace goes roughly as follows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Recall that a sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $Z\in2^{\omega}$
\end_inset

 is said to be 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

-random if it passes every Martin-Löf test,
 that is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
For every uniform sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\{U_{k}\}_{k\in\N}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}$
\end_inset

 classes,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu U_{k}\leq2^{-k}$
\end_inset

,
 there is some 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $Z\notin U_{N}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Prompt the audience to generalize the 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 into an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

.
 Here are the ways I want to come up:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Uniform sequence of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 classes,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n-1)}$
\end_inset

-uniform sequence of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 classes,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Uniform sequence of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}(0^{(n-1)})$
\end_inset

 classes,
 AKA uniform sequence of 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n-1)}$
\end_inset

-effective opens
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Uniform sequence of 
\emph on
open
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 classes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n-1)}$
\end_inset

-uniform sequence of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}$
\end_inset

 classes?
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
That last one is a bit of an exception.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

,
 it actually corresponds to the notion of weak 2-randomness,
 and neither Denis nor myself know exactly what happens for 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq3$
\end_inset

,
 but it is definitely at most as strong as weak 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-randomness.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Our goal today,
 and indeed the goal of Theorem 6.8.3,
 is to show that all of these agree (aside from the weird exception).
 But first,
 I want to talk about 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 classes,
 because there was some amount of confusion that arose in me when I was studying this subject,
 that was also displayed by at least one person in this class:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
By Post's theorem,
 wasn't 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 the same as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}(0^{(n-1)})$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The answer is no!
 And one of the things I'll do today is prove that.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
But first,
 I want to do some set up to justify the following fact:
 There are a bunch of things being named 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 that are different things but actually the same thing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Effective Topological Spaces
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Let's talk about topological spaces in some generality.
 Here are some examples:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\N$
\end_inset

,
 or indeed any countable set,
 with the discrete topology,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $2^{\omega}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\R$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Each of these spaces comes equipped with a computable basis.
 It's unclear what 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

computable
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 means,
 outside of natural examples.
 I did some research on the matter,
 but there doesn't seem to be a unified general notion of what is meant by 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

computable topological space
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
One of the most successful notions is that of 
\emph on
effective polish space
\emph default
,
 which I believe Subhasish might introduce us to?
 But there are other competing notions.
 See:
 Hoyrup,
 Melnikov,
 Ng,
 2024.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Nevertheless,
 for any reasonable space,
 we can find a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

computable basis
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 etc.
 A 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

general open
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 will be an arbitrary union of basis elements,
 while an 
\emph on
effective open
\emph default
 will be a computably enumerable union of basis elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Some properties we might want would be that,
 e.g.,
 an intersection of two effective opens will itself be an effective open.
 This requires that the intersection of 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 be uniformly written as a union of other basis elements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
If we demand that our computable basis elements are all nonempty,
 we obtain a notion that Hoyrup et al.
\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset

refer to as 
\emph on
computable topological presentation
\emph default
 of a space.
 They study this notion and its relation with other notions in the literature at some length.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The main thing is that,
 once we have a notion of 
\emph on
effective open
\emph default
,
 we can start to consider other effective types of sets.
 Effective closed sets,
 for example.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Definition:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\{B_{i}\}_{i\in\N}$
\end_inset

 is an effective basis of the topological space 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 we define a subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 to be
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Effectively open,
 AKA 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 if it's of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\bigcup_{n\in W_{e}}B_{n}$
\end_inset

 for some c.e.
\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset

set 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{e}$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Effectively closed,
 AKA 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 if it's the complement of an effective open,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{2}$
\end_inset

 if it's of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\bigcap_{n\in W_{e}}\bigcup_{m\in W_{n}}B_{m}$
\end_inset

,
 etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
To be fully general,
 define a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}$
\end_inset

 subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 to be an effective open,
 define 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{n}$
\end_inset

 to be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

complement of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 and define 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n+1}$
\end_inset

 as a countable effective union of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
If you apply this notion to the three examples above,
 you get:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Notions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{n}$
\end_inset

 subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $\N$
\end_inset

.
 These are the ones you probably studied in computability theory.
 The basic opens in this case are singletons.
 I will henceforth refer to these as 
\emph on
sets
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Notions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{n}$
\end_inset

 subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{\omega}$
\end_inset

.
 The basic opens in this case are strings.
 These I will refer to as 
\emph on
classes
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Notions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{n}$
\end_inset

 subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $\R$
\end_inset

.
 The basic opens in this case are open intervals.
 I would also refer to these as classes,
 but they won't feature in the rest of this talk.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Post's Theorem,
 and Not
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
All of the previous notions relativize obviously.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem (Post):
 A 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\N$
\end_inset

 is the same as a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}(0^{(n-1)})$
\end_inset

 subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\N$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Proof sketch:
 A 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 subset is one of the form:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup\neg\cup\neg\cup\dots\neg\cup B_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg$
\end_inset

 denotes complementation,
 and all of the unions are c.e.
 Now,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup B_{i}$
\end_inset

 is c.e.,
 hence 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg\cup B_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable.
 Hence,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup\neg\cup B_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-c.e.,
 which is the same as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}(0^{'})$
\end_inset

.
 Next,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg\cup\neg\cup B_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $0''$
\end_inset

-computable,
 whence 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup\neg\cup\neg\cup B_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $0''$
\end_inset

-c.e,
 i.e.
\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}(0'')$
\end_inset

.
 And so on.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
For the other direction:
 We show that a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}(0')$
\end_inset

 set is a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n+1}$
\end_inset

 set.
 By relativization and induction,
 this proves the general result.
 Now,
 a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}(0')$
\end_inset

 set is of the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\cup\neg\cup\dots\neg\cup B_{i},
\]

\end_inset

where each step is 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-enumerable.
 Now,
 we can do a trick where we push all 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computability onto the last union as follows:
 Make all but the last union be over all natural numbers,
 and then the last one enumerates 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 iff all the other ones 
\emph on
would
\emph default
 be enumerated,
 and in turn 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is as well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Concrete example:
 Consider 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup_{n\in W_{e}^{0'}}\neg\cup_{m\in W_{n}^{0'}}B_{m}$
\end_inset

.
 Replace it by 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup_{n\in\N}\neg\cup_{m\in W_{f(n)}^{0'}}B_{m}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(n)$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-program:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

check if 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is enumerated into 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{e}^{0'}$
\end_inset

,
 and if so,
 proceed to enumerate 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{n}^{0'}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Thus,
 it suffices to establish that every 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}(0')$
\end_inset

 set is a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 set.
 So,
 consider 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup_{n\in W_{e}^{0'}}B_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 Recall that we can computably approximate 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{e}^{0'}$
\end_inset

 as follows:
 Enumerate things using an approximation of 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

 as an oracle,
 and sometimes change your mind because you realize that you enumerated someone only because you made a mistake.
 In this way,
 you can see 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{e}^{0'}$
\end_inset

 as a computable sequence of sequences 
\begin_inset Formula $q(n,s)$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in W_{e}^{0'}$
\end_inset

 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $q(n,s)\to1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\cup_{n\in W_{e}^{0'}}B_{n}=\cup_{\braket{n,t}\in\N^{2}}\cap_{s\geq t}\begin{cases}
B_{n} & q(n,s)=1,\\
\emptyset & q(n,s)\neq1.
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

In other words,
 we just wrote an arbitrary 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}(0')$
\end_inset

 set as a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Proof complete.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Now,
 one of these directions is fully general,
 while the other does require very specifically that we work with the natural numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem (not Post):
 In a generic setting,
 every 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}(0^{(n-1)})$
\end_inset

 class is a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 class.
 However,
 the converse need not hold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Note:
 In fact,
 every 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{m}(0^{(n)})$
\end_inset

 class is a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n+m}$
\end_inset

 class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Let's show that the converse of Post's theorem does not hold in 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{\omega}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
We do this by making the following remark:
 There is a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 class that is not open,
 namely 
\begin_inset Formula $\{0^{\omega}\}$
\end_inset

.
 In contrast,
 every 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}(X)$
\end_inset

 class is always open.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Details:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\{0^{\omega}\}=\cap_{n\in\N}\llbracket0^{n}\rrbracket$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{1}$
\end_inset

 class,
 hence trivially a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
This argument will work,
 in fact,
 in any space that contains an effective closed that is not open.
 This does not work in 
\begin_inset Formula $\N$
\end_inset

 because in the discrete topology everything is open.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
This does bring up the following question:
 Is every 
\emph on
open
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 class a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}(0^{(n-1)})$
\end_inset

 class?
 The answer turns out to still be no at level 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
The proof is technical and I have more things I want to talk about so I'm not going to give it.
 There's a proof in Denis' book,
 but they refer to a lemma in another resource.
 I have a self-contained proof written up in my webpage,
 and if anyone's interested I'll be happy to share it.
 It has pictures!
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
If I have time at the end I'll sketch the proof.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
In summary,
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq1$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Sigma_{n}\text{ class}\implies\text{open \ensuremath{\Sigma_{n}} class}\implies0^{(n-1)}\text{-effective open},
\]

\end_inset

and all of these implications are strict in 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{\omega}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Approximations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
With this in mind,
 we can define three distinct notions of relativized ML tests,
 with three 
\emph on
a priori
\emph default
 distinct notions of randomness.
 The notion with 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n-1)}$
\end_inset

-effective opens is the 
\emph on
a priori
\emph default
 weakest (less tests,
 more randoms),
 while the one with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 classes is 
\shape italic
a priori
\shape default
 strongest (most tests).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The definition with effective opens is my favorite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Anyway,
 it turns out that these three notions are,
 in fact,
 equivalent,
 resulting in the notion of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-randomness.
 My next goal is to prove this fact.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Here's the idea:
 Even though,
 as we saw,
 there are 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 classes that are not 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n-1)}$
\end_inset

-effective opens,
 every 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 class can be approximated from above by such opens.
 If we prove this 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

effectively enough
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 we can imagine the following procedure.
 From a sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{n}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 classes of exponentially decaying measure 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq2^{-n}$
\end_inset

,
 build another sequence of 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n-1)}$
\end_inset

-effective opens 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{n}\supseteq S_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu U_{n}\leq\mu S_{n}+2^{-n}\leq2^{-(n-1)}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 aside from a small off-by-one error,
 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{n}$
\end_inset

 induces a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

strong
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 test.
 Hence,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 is random in the sense of effective opens,
 it will fail to be in some 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 hence some 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 and thus will also be random in the sense of classes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
And this,
 here,
 is the content of Theorem 6.8.3:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Theorem:
 Any 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 class can be effectively approximated from above by 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n-1)}$
\end_inset

-effective opens.
 More precisely,
 given an index for a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 class 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

,
 and a rational 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon>0$
\end_inset

,
 we can effectively compute an index for a 
\begin_inset Formula $0^{(n-1)}$
\end_inset

-effective open 
\begin_inset Formula $U\supseteq S$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu U\leq\mu S+\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
(The actual Theorem 6.8.3 has a ton of other bells and whistles that I don't want to talk about.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Instead of doing the proof for general 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 let's do something that I think is more enlightening:
 We shall prove it for a few values of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 and then hopefully it will be clear to everyone how one 
\emph on
could
\emph default
 induct if they wanted to.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Case 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

:
 Definition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Case 
\begin_inset Formula $n=2$
\end_inset

:
 A 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 class is of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup_{i\in\N}\neg\cup_{j\in W_{f(i)}}B_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

 wants to approximate this from above with an open,
 it suffices to approximate each 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg\cup_{j\in W_{f(i)}}B_{j}$
\end_inset

 from above with an open to arbitrary precision,
 then do the 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=2^{-i}\varepsilon$
\end_inset

 trick.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Now,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg\cup_{j\in W}B_{j}$
\end_inset

 is a complement of an open...
 But here is a crucial trick:
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 are clopens!
 This means that we can approximate it from above by simply stopping the enumeration at some point,
 and then enumerating the complement of what we've got.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Finally,
 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

 is good at calculating measures of these things.
 In particular,
 it can answer the question:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

am I ever going to add more than 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 measure starting now?
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Eventually,
 the answer shall be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (because finite measure space).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
So,
 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

 can just figure out where to stop for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
 and enumerate the complement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Remark:
 If you want to prove this lemma in some kind of general topological space,
 you'll need some extra property like:
 For each basic open 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 we can uniformly compute a union of basic opens that covers 
\begin_inset Formula $X\setminus B$
\end_inset

 within measure 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
I have not been able to find any data on this condition.
 It is certainly true for 
\begin_inset Formula $\interval 01$
\end_inset

,
 for example.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Case 
\begin_inset Formula $n=3$
\end_inset

:
 A 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{3}$
\end_inset

 class is of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup_{i\in\N}\neg\cup_{j\in\N}\neg\cup_{k\in W_{f(i,j)}}B_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Now,
 we saw that 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

 is capable of approximating 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup_{j\in\N}\cdots$
\end_inset

 from above to arbitrary measure,
 but that's actually not useful right now,
 because the complement turns that into an approximation from below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Let's rewrite the problem a slightly different way:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup_{i\in\N}\cap_{j\in\N}\cup_{k\in W_{f(i,j)}}B_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 we want to approximate 
\begin_inset Formula $\cap_{j\in\N}\cdots$
\end_inset

 from above to arbitrary measure,
 using 
\begin_inset Formula $0''$
\end_inset

.
 Maybe a reasonable way to do this is to cut the intersection off at a finite point:
 This will give an open set (yay),
 an effective one at that (requires intersection trick),
 and at some point you're definitely within 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 measure,
 so it suffices to find out how 
\begin_inset Formula $0''$
\end_inset

 can figure out the cutoff point.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
So,
 the set 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{n}=\cap_{j\leq n}\cdots$
\end_inset

 is an effective open,
 right?
 Thus,
 there's a computer program that approximates its measure from below.
 Then,
 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

 can find an approximation of its measure within 
\begin_inset Formula $1/n$
\end_inset

,
 say.
 Call that 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 the true measures 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu G_{n}$
\end_inset

 are decreasing to the real measure 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu G$
\end_inset

,
 and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{n}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable sequence that converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu G$
\end_inset

.
 Hence,
 
\begin_inset Formula $0''$
\end_inset

 can ask the question 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

is 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{n}$
\end_inset

 ever going to be more than 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 different from what it is now?
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 and if the answer is no (which it eventually will be),
 your 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{n}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

-approximation of the real measure,
 and indeed so is every subsequent 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 (May be strictness shenanigans.) By taking 
\begin_inset Formula $n>1/\delta$
\end_inset

,
 we get a 
\begin_inset Formula $2\delta$
\end_inset

-approximation,
 but it's okay.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Case 
\begin_inset Formula $n=4$
\end_inset

:
 A 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{4}$
\end_inset

 class is of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup_{i}\neg\cup_{j}\neg\cup_{k}\neg\cup_{\ell\in W_{f(i,j,k)}}B_{\ell}$
\end_inset

.
 Now,
 we can actually piggyback on prior results:
 This is 
\begin_inset Formula $\cup_{i}\cap_{j}\text{a \ensuremath{\Sigma_{2}} class}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 we can approximate the 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 class uniformly from above by a 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable open within desired measure – 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{-i-j}\varepsilon$
\end_inset

 works.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
Details on that:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\cap_{j}(A_{j}\cup E_{j})$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu E_{j}\leq2^{-j}\delta$
\end_inset

 can be bounded as a set by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\cap_{j}A_{j}\right)\cup\left(\cup_{j}E_{j}\right)$
\end_inset

 bc any element of the former is either in some 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 or it's in all 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus the measure accrued is at most 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $2\delta$
\end_inset

 or wtv.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Then,
 what we get is actually a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{3}(0')$
\end_inset

 class,
 with excess measure of something like 
\begin_inset Formula $4\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 we use the relativized version of the result for 
\begin_inset Formula $n=3$
\end_inset

 to get a 
\begin_inset Formula $(0')''=0'''$
\end_inset

 uniform open above 
\emph on
that
\emph default
 with excess measure 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

,
 and thus we've computed* a 
\begin_inset Formula $0'''$
\end_inset

-uniform open above our original 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{4}$
\end_inset

 class with excess measure 
\begin_inset Formula $5\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Big asterisk:
 Actually,
 we only 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computed!!!
 Fortunately,
 this is OK by the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

push computation forward
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 trick:
 Given a way to 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

-compute an index for an 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

-effective open,
 you can always turn that into a way to compute an index for the same 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

-effective open,
 by having part of the program that enumerates the open sets start with asking 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 to compute the index and then enumerate the corresponding open.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
In other words,
 this theorem relativizes as:
 Given an index for a 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}(X)$
\end_inset

 class 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon>0$
\end_inset

,
 you can 
\emph on
computably
\emph default
 (not just 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

-computably) obtain an index for an 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{(n-1)}$
\end_inset

-effective open 
\begin_inset Formula $U\supseteq S$
\end_inset

 with excess measure less than 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
From here,
 the structure of the proof for general 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 should be relatively clear,
 though it's worth noting that it will depend on parity.
 This is because,
 in fact,
 we are 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 inducting in 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 but actually doing an alternating induction in the arithmetic hierarchy 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{1}\to\Pi_{2}\to\Sigma_{3}\to\cdots$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{1}\to\Sigma_{2}\to\Pi_{3}\to\Sigma_{4}\to\cdots$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout point
So,
 even though we're actually only interested upper approximations of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{n}$
\end_inset

 classes,
 we're being forced to go through upper approximations of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{n}$
\end_inset

 classes as well.
 I think that's kind of interesting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
An Open 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 Class That is Not a 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-Effective Open
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Time allowing,
 here is a construction of an open 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 class that is not a 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-effective open.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The set itself will be very simple:
 It shall be 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{\omega}\setminus\{A\}$
\end_inset

,
 for a well-chosen element 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 This is evidently open.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
The set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 shall come from the following lemma.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Lemma:
 There is a computable binary tree with exactly one path that does not terminate in zeros.
 This path is not 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Remark:
 This is actually a bastardized version of a more natural statement for Baire space:
 There is a computable infinitely-branching tree with exactly one path,
 which is not 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Proof sketch:
 In the Baire version,
 diagonalize against the 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-th 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable function in the 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-th layer.
 Do this by using a computable approximation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable functions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
At each stage,
 we think that we should be diagonalizing against some branch.
 So,
 we pick some branch that's not that one,
 and grow the tree starting there.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{n}(n)\iswd$
\end_inset

,
 this choice will change finitely many times,
 so the first layer will only have one child.
 Good.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
On the other hand,
 we must think about what happens if 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{n}(n)\isnotwd$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.
\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset

the computable approximation will change its mind infinitely many times.
 We need to ensure that there is a child that 
\emph on
will
\emph default
 grow infinitely much,
 in this case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
So,
 here's the idea:
 Whenever you change your mind about 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{n}(n)$
\end_inset

,
 you go back to growing the zeroth tree for a little bit.
 This requires a bit of technical care.
 But it works.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
To go from this construction (a computable infinitely-branching tree with only one path,
 which is not 
\begin_inset Formula $0'$
\end_inset

-computable) to the set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 we do the diagonal-y thing to turn an infinitely branching tree into a binary one,
 say 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
 This adds a bunch of paths that end in infinitely many zeros.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Point
Anyway,
 once you have the resulting 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in2^{\omega}$
\end_inset

,
 you consider the 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{2}$
\end_inset

 class given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
2^{\omega}\setminus\{A\}=\{\,X\in2^{\omega}\mid
\]

